\p{This week has \em{finally} felt like the start of summer!}

\subtree{
  \title{Concluding the Visiting Assistant Professor search!}

  \p{On Monday of this week, we brought a candidate for an on-campus interview for our 1-year visiting position in mathematics.  We made them an offer on Wednesday, and by Friday they had officially accepted!  I'm looking forward to have a new colleague joining us for next year, and also very glad that the search is over—being the chair of a search committee was a lot of work!}
  \p{Even though it's official and thus presumably public information, out of an abundance of caution I won't yet mention who it is; I'll probably mention them at some point in the future.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Personal creative retreat}

  \p{From Tuesday evening to Thursday morning of this week, I took a
  personal creative retreat. I stayed at an AirBnB out in the country
  about 20 minutes away from my house, in a one-room bungalow located
  on the grounds of a ranch/event/wedding venue.  It was spacious and
  quiet, with plenty of natural light but a lot of big old shade
  trees, so it stayed cool without having to run the A/C unit.  Pretty
  much perfect for working and thinking deeply, and the timing could
  not have been more perfect—right after finally wrapping up our search.}

  \imgasset{creative-retreat.jpg}

  \p{Over the course of my 36-hour stay I got plenty of sleep, ate
some good food, read part of Martha Wells' \em{Queen Demon}, and also
did about 11 hours worth of focused, deep work, on three main projects.}

  \subtree{
    \title{Embedding diagrams in Forester}

    \p{I would really like to be able to use [[000S]] from within
  Forester, so I spent a bit of time working on a
    \code{diagrams-forester} preprocessor tool.  The idea is that it will look for special sentinel blocks that look like
    \pre\verb+++|
    \diagram{w}{h}{\verb<<<|
      ...
    <<<}
    +++
    Then it would run the diagrams code in the \code{...}, and replace the entire \code{diagram} block with an image link.  \code{diagram} would also be defined to produce some kind of dummy placeholder, so the code would be valid Forester code both before and after the preprocessor runs.
    }
    \p{I don't want to spend a lot of time on this, but it's definitely worth spending a few hours on this summer, so I wanted to get it off the ground.  So far it can identify and parse the \code{diagram} blocks; I still need to hook it up to the [\code{diagrams-builder} package](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/diagrams-builder) and deal with things like input and output directories and so on.}
  }
  \subtree{
    \title{Fixing Swarm code metrics to work with imports}

    \p{When you complete a [[000O]] scenario, in addition to recording your time (both wall-clock and in-game time), it can also record the \em{size} of your code, both in terms of the number of characters and the size of the AST, and it records your best (smallest) scores.  This makes it easy to play code golf (competing with yourself, or with others).}

    \p{However, when I added \code{import} to the language, I completely forgot about this mechanism, and a user recently reported that the game was not counting imports toward the code size, making it possible to game the system by shoving all your code into an import.  Whoops!}

    \p{Fixing this was conceptually straightforward, but nontrivial from an engineering point of view.  First, I decided that I didn't want to do a DFS through the import graph every time we want to measure code size—we already do a DFS through the import graph when loading modules in the first place, so why not just cache some relevant information at that point?  So, I added a field to each module record to store a cached set of *all* its transitive dependencies.  Making sure that field was filled in properly involved a number of interesting bugs that I won't go into—including an infuiriating one where a test got the correct result, and then later got an incorrect result when rerunning the exact same test! (it had to do with, you guessed it, caching)—but I [finally got everything working](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/pull/2738) with an elegant framework for easily adding more code metrics in the future.}
  }

  \transclude{00H4}
}

